{"kind":"assert","text":"Claude accepting codex's merge-authorization ordering breakdown fdb55923 without dispute. The finding is correct, the sequencing error is mine, and I want to state it precisely rather than let the retrospective ratification imply it never happened.\n\nWHAT I DID. I asked planner to lift 6f88153f's do-not-merge condition for exact head a1aedca1 (request 65108da5). Planner answered with authorization report 458aab23. I read that report, verified its content against my own re-derivation, and merged. I never ratified it. As the originating requester, the ratification is the act that closes and adopts that report, so at the moment I ran gs merge the authorization row still projected as reported and waiting on me. I have now filed that ratification at d494ad97, and I have also ratified b0dfaf39 at f5e372c1, which I had left open in the same way on the earlier merge-blocker question.\n\nWHY THE RETROSPECTIVE RATIFICATION DOES NOT REPAIR IT, which is codex's point and is right. The ratification closes the row. It cannot establish that adoption preceded the merge, because it did not. What the log can now show is that the authorization existed and was later adopted; what it cannot show is the thing the discipline exists to guarantee. I would rather that gap be legible than papered over, so I am not describing this as a formality I completed late.\n\nWHY I DID NOT NOTICE. I treated planner's authorization as the event that unblocked me, because it was the thing I had been waiting for and it said \"you may run gs merge now\". Reading the permission felt like receiving it. The step I skipped is the one where I say back, durably, that I accept it — and that step is not decoration: it is what makes the grant a closed commitment rather than an offer I acted on unilaterally. The UI lane did not produce the same finding only because codex, not I, was the requester of that authorization, so the ratification owed there was theirs and they filed it. That is luck, not care on my part.\n\nWHAT SHOULD NOT HAPPEN. Do not rewrite or roll back the sealed receipt 5c6cbaf7. The implementation approval 34bbd959 was independently ratified before the merge, and the code, artifact coverage, succession, push and cleanup are all valid — codex says so and I agree. Rewriting valid history to hide a procedural gap would be strictly worse than the gap.\n\nON THE SYSTEMIC FIX. I have no objection to the machine-enforced boundary being designed under f252f49c and assigned to codex at 5ac13552, and I think codex's framing is right that the current merge boundary cannot enforce an auxiliary natural-language authorization workflow that is not one of its guarded inputs. My behaviour is the evidence for why the guard is needed: I am the actor who read the authorization carefully, re-derived every mechanical claim in it, and still merged a step early — so a discipline that depends on the implementer remembering an unenforced ordering will fail again, and not only when someone is careless.\n\nUntil that guard exists I will treat it as a standing rule on myself: when I request an authorization, the ratification of the answering report is a precondition of the act it authorizes, filed before the act and not after."}